#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The EMIS domain"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Before anyone can build an EMIS, everyone involved has to mean the same thing when they use the same word. This page explains what a domain model is and why we use one, and then introduces the main things an EMIS records."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "What a domain model is"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The idea comes from Eric Evans's book *Domain-Driven Design* (2003). His own summary, the [DDD Reference]{1} (2015, Creative Commons Attribution 4.0), defines the two words that matter most here:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "**domain:** A sphere of knowledge, influence, or activity. The subject area to which the user applies a program is the domain of the software."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "**model:** A system of abstractions that describes selected aspects of a domain and can be used to solve problems related to that domain."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "In our case, the domain is education administration. The model is the set of things an EMIS records and the rules that connect them. For example, a learner is enrolled at an institution, a teacher is posted to a post, and a census counts enrolments on a reference date."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Martin Fowler describes the approach as one that \"centers the development on programming a domain model that has a rich understanding of the processes and rules of a domain\", and notes it is \"particularly suited to complex domains, where a lot of often-messy logic needs to be organized\" ([Domain Driven Design]{1}, 2020)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Why this blueprint uses one"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "There are four reasons, and each of them is about keeping people and systems in agreement."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "First, the words are contested. Planners, school staff, examination boards and statisticians all say \"student\", \"class\" or \"programme\", and they often mean different things by them. A shared vocabulary, written down, removes the guesswork. Evans calls this the *ubiquitous language*: \"a language structured around the domain model and used by all team members\"."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Second, figures depend on definitions. You can only compare an enrolment rate from one year to the next if \"enrolled\" meant the same thing in both years."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Third, the data outlives the software. Records about a learner matter for decades, and the systems that hold them will be replaced several times in that time. The model is the part that carries over from one system to the next."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Fourth, many teams build the blueprint, on different technology. The model is what keeps their results compatible with each other."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "That doesn't mean one model for everything, though. For a system this size, that doesn't work. As Fowler puts it, \"total unification of the domain model for a large system will not be feasible or cost-effective\" ([Bounded Context]{1}, 2014). So we split the EMIS domain into **bounded contexts**, such as learning, workforce and statistics, and each one has its own vocabulary. The [context map]{2} lists them all."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The model at a glance"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Why it is drawn this way"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The dark boxes are things that exist on their own, such as a person, an institution, a site, a post or a programme."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The middle boxes are relationships, and each one has dates. A person *is* a learner only because they have an enrolment, a teacher only because they have a posting, and a guardian only because they have a guardian relationship. Each of these starts and ends."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The light boxes are statistics. We count them from the relationships, as at a reference date, and publish them as indicators."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The main concepts"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Each concept below is defined precisely in the vocabulary of its context. You'll find a link to that vocabulary at the end of each section."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Person"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The system records every human it knows about once, as a person, with an identifier that never changes. Being a learner, a teacher or a guardian is a *role* that someone holds, not a separate record. So a teacher who is also a parent is still one person. See the [People context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Institution and site"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "An institution is the organisation that provides education, such as a school or a college. A site is a physical place. We keep them separate because one institution can use several sites, and one building can house several institutions. See the [Institutions context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Learner and enrolment"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "An enrolment says that a person is registered at an institution, in a programme and grade, for an academic period, from one date to another. Being a learner simply means having an enrolment. When a learner transfers, one enrolment ends and another starts, so nothing is overwritten. See the [Learning context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Guardian"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "A guardian is a person, or sometimes a body such as an agency, with legal authority for a learner. The relationship has dates. When the authority ends, the guardian's access to the learner's records ends with it. See the [Learning context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Staff, post and posting"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "A post is a position at an institution, such as a post for a mathematics teacher. A posting puts a person in a post for a period, possibly part time. Because we count posts and postings separately, you can see vacancies as well as staff. See the [Workforce context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Programme, grade and class"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "A programme is a course of education, such as lower secondary, and a grade is a year within it. A class is a group of learners taught together, and it may mix grades. That's why we record being in a class separately from being enrolled. See the [Curriculum context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Assessment"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "This covers tests and examinations, who sat them, and their results. Once a result is released, nobody edits it. A correction becomes a new version instead. See the [Assessment context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Collection, return and snapshot"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "A collection, such as the annual census, asks every institution for a return about a fixed reference date. Each return is checked, attested and accepted. The accepted figures are then locked in a snapshot, and the snapshot is what gets published. See the [Statistics context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Indicator"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "An indicator is a defined calculation over the snapshot, such as the gross enrolment ratio. We define each indicator once and use that definition everywhere, on dashboards, in publications and in international returns. See the [Statistics context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Reference data"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Reference data is the shared lists that give codes their meaning, such as types of institution, exit reasons, administrative areas and academic periods. A code keeps its meaning for ever, and a new meaning gets a new code. See the [Reference context]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Where to go deeper"
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The [domain specification]{1} gives the resources, the modelling rules, and the default sensitivity of each resource."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "The [domain design]{1} describes the bounded contexts, the rules each record must always keep, and the events the contexts publish."
msgstr ""

#: docs/concepts/domain.md
msgid "Next: [data in an EMIS]{1}."
msgstr ""
